  <h2>🥋 Yiquan / Dachengquan Tradition</h2>
  <p>Master Lam's Zhan Zhuang system traces its lineage to <strong>Wang Xiangzhai</strong>, who founded
  <strong>Yiquan</strong> (also known as <strong>Dachengquan</strong>) in the early 20th century.
  Wang Xiangzhai distilled the essential training methods of traditional Chinese internal martial arts into
  a system centered on standing meditation as its core practice. His student <strong>Professor Yu Yongnian</strong>
  further developed and documented the health benefits of the system, and Master Lam studied under him.</p>
  <p>To explore the Yiquan / Dachengquan tradition further, search for legitimately published books on
  Amazon or other bookstores using terms like <em>"Yiquan"</em>, <em>"Dachengquan"</em>,
  <em>"Wang Xiangzhai"</em>, or <em>"standing qigong"</em>.</p>

  ---

  <h2>🔬 Scientific Research</h2>
  <p>Modern scientific research has begun to investigate the mechanisms behind Zhan Zhuang and related practices.
  To find published studies, search <strong>PubMed</strong> or <strong>Google Scholar</strong> for terms such as:</p>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li><code>"standing meditation"</code></li>
    <li><code>"zhan zhuang"</code></li>
    <li><code>"qigong"</code></li>
    <li><code>"standing qigong"</code></li>
    <li><code>"static postural training"</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Key areas of research include:</p>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li><strong>Neuroimaging (fNIRS)</strong>: Functional near-infrared spectroscopy studies show unique prefrontal cortex activity during standing meditation, producing a distinctive "alert relaxation" neural signature</li>
    <li><strong>Heart Rate Variability (HRV)</strong>: Studies report improved HRV and increased parasympathetic nervous system activity, indicating better autonomic regulation</li>
    <li><strong>Interoception & Proprioception</strong>: Enhanced body awareness and proprioceptive acuity from sustained static postures</li>
    <li><strong>Static Loading</strong>: Musculoskeletal adaptations that differ from dynamic exercise — building postural stability and endurance through isometric load</li>
  </ul>

  ---

  <h2>🧘 Related Practices</h2>
  <p>Zhan Zhuang can complement other traditional Chinese mind-body practices:</p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Baduanjin (Eight Brocades)</h3>
      <p>A set of eight gentle qigong movements that pair well with Zhan Zhuang as warm-ups or complementary practice. Search for instructional books and videos online.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Tai Chi (Taijiquan)</h3>
      <p>An internal martial art sharing the same principles of sung (relaxation), chi cultivation, and mind-body integration. Many practitioners find Zhan Zhuang deepens their Tai Chi practice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Qigong</h3>
      <p>A broad family of energy cultivation practices. Zhan Zhuang is itself a form of qigong — specifically "standing qigong." Moving qigong forms can complement the static practice.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

